NUM-16: keep the notch flow of a partly open V-notch weir

A V-notch weir with a setting below 1 is computed as a trapezoidal weir
whose crest is raised by (1 - setting) * height. The triangular ends of that
trapezoid are the notch itself, but their flow used Cd2, the end coefficient
of TRAPEZOIDAL weirs, which defaults to 0 and is not documented for V-notch
weirs. A setting of 0.999 therefore removed almost all of the weir's flow.
In StructureSolver::computeWeirFlowK, use the V-notch coefficient (Cd, or its Cd curve value) for the ends of a
partly open V-notch weir; the rectangular centre strip is unchanged. Fully
open V-notch weirs and all other weir types are unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-16_swmm600.patch

diff --git a/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp b/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
index 77d61bf..2642f4a 100644
--- a/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
+++ b/src/engine/hydraulics/HydStructures.cpp
@@ -961,7 +961,8 @@ void StructureSolver::computeWeirFlowK(SimulationContext& ctx,
                         ? 2.0 * weirs_.slope[uk] * y
                         : links.xsect_y_bot[uj] + 2.0 * weirs_.slope[uk] * y;
                     q1_out = cd * (w * ucfL) * std::pow(h, 1.5);
-                    q2_out = cd2 * weirs_.slope[uk] * std::pow(h, 2.5);
+                    // a partly open V-notch keeps its own Cd for the notch
+                    q2_out = (wt == 2 ? cd : cd2) * weirs_.slope[uk] * std::pow(h, 2.5);
                     break;
                 }
             }
